\begin_layout Subsubsection
Packet Structure
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The packet consisted of a header (12 bytes) which had a version number,
 device ID,
 sequence number and payload length.
 The header was structured in the following manner
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Header (>BBIIH format string for struct.pack() and struct.unpack() in Python:
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
> = big-endian byte order (for network transmission)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
B = 1 byte = version number
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
B = 1 byte = flags
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
I = 4 bytes = device id
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
I = 4 bytes = sequence number
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
H = 2 bytes = payload length
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
OSC Payload = 28 bytes
\end_layout

\begin_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
One of the strings we are transmitting is
\family typewriter
 /controller/pot1
\family default
.
 This is 16 characters.
 But OSC has to be null-terminated,
 so this becomes 17 bytes.
 I padded this to 20 bytes.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
OSC's type tag string,
 e.g.
 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

,f
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

.
 This is 2 characters plus a null terminator,
 so 3 bytes.
 Padded to 4 bytes.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
The float argument in OSC is a 32-bit float,
 which adds 4 bytes.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Altogether this sums to 28 bytes for the entire OSC payload.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Now let's examine what happens if we transmit 
\family typewriter
/controller/button1
\family default
.
 This sums to 19 characters,
 plus a null terminator = 20.
 This is already a multiple of 4,
 so it doesn't need padding.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
The type tag string for this is 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

,i
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

,
 which is 2 characters plus the obligatory null terminator,
 which (when padded) again yields 4 butes.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
OSC integers are 32 bits,
 so this is 4 bytes.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
The total payload size in this case is also 28 bytes.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
If I were to have a control string such as 
\family typewriter
/controller/button10
\family default
,
 I would have to rethink the payload length.
\end_layout

\end_deeper
\begin_layout Enumerate
Finally we have the HMAC-SHA256 tag.
 A full SHA-256 hash is 32 bytes,
 but I truncated it to 16 (which still gives 
\begin_inset Formula $2^{128}$
\end_inset

 possible tags,
 which is secure enough for my needs.
 The keyed hash is computed over the header AND the payload.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Since 12 + 28 + 16 = 56,
 the total size of each packet is 56 bytes.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Authentication Mechanism
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
This is HMAC-SHA256 over (header || OSC payload).
 This is truncated to 16 bytes.
 The key is NOT transmitted and is shared out of band.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsubsection
Replay Protection
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Monotonically increasing sequence numbers.
 Receiver rejects duplicates or out-of-order packets (depending on policy).
\end_layout

